WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:13.035
<v Maya>So if you're listening right now, I want you to just imagine for a second if every single software bug could just be mathematically proven out of existence, like completely eliminated before an app is even installed on your phone.</v>

2
00:00:13.395 --> 00:00:15.156
<v Daniel>Right. Which sounds totally like science fiction.</v>

3
00:00:15.296 --> 00:00:29.941
<v Maya>Yeah, exactly. It sounds impossible. But today's deep dive is exploring a massive leap toward that actually becoming reality. We're looking at a brand new technical paper. It was just released on November 10th, 2026 by Google DeepMind and Google.</v>

4
00:00:30.365 --> 00:00:35.108
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's called Alpha Proof Code, Verifying Signal Security at the Machine Code Level.</v>

5
00:00:35.248 --> 00:00:50.236
<v Maya>Right. And our mission today is really to just unpack this paper. We want to understand how they basically used an AI swarm to mathematically prove the security of the actual compiled machine code of the signal</v>

6
00:00:50.276 --> 00:00:54.418
<v Maya>messaging app. And, you know, what this means for the future of software in general.</v>

7
00:00:54.698 --> 00:01:08.047
<v Daniel>It's a huge deal. I mean, to set the stakes here, Signal protects the communications of literally billions of people. And it's not just the Signal app itself. The underlying cryptography is used in WhatsApp, Facebook Messenger, Google Messages.</v>

8
00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:09.408
<v Maya>So basically everyone's phones.</v>

9
00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:20.356
<v Daniel>Exactly. And historically, this idea of formal verification, which is basically mathematically proving that code has absolutely no bugs, it took years of human labor.</v>

10
00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:21.857
<v Maya>Right, like decades sometimes.</v>

11
00:01:22.225 --> 00:01:30.339
<v Daniel>Yeah. But this new paper claims to have done it for a real-world, widely deployed cryptographic protocol. And they did it using AI.</v>

12
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:44.032
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this. Because the central tension here for me, and I'm sure for anyone listening who follows AI, is like, how on earth do we trust an AI to check critical security code when AI is, you know, notoriously good at hallucinating?</v>

13
00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:45.974
<v Daniel>That is the million-dollar question.</v>

14
00:01:46.455 --> 00:01:46.655
<v Maya>Right.</v>

15
00:01:46.975 --> 00:02:01.709
<v Daniel>But before we get to how the AI didn't cheat, we need to talk about why verifying the machine code is such a breakthrough. So just briefly for context, Signal relies on a couple of core components. There's the PQXDH,</v>

16
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:03.071
<v Daniel>post-quantum handshake.</v>

17
00:02:03.464 --> 00:02:06.525
<v Maya>Which is how the two phones agree on a secret key to start with, right?</v>

18
00:02:06.845 --> 00:02:14.667
<v Daniel>Exactly. And it's designed to be secure even if quantum computers become a thing later. And then once that's set up, you have the triple ratchet.</v>

19
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:18.388
<v Maya>Which is the double ratchet plus the sparse post-quantum ratchet.</v>

20
00:02:18.508 --> 00:02:30.250
<v Daniel>You got it. And the ratchet drives fresh keys for every single message you send. This gives you two vital concepts. First is forward secrecy. So if someone steals your current key, they can't go back and read your past messages.</v>

21
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.831
<v Maya>Because those older keys are just gone.</v>

22
00:02:31.931 --> 00:02:42.519
<v Daniel>Exactly. And the second concept is post-compromise security. If someone does steal your key, you actually lock them out again as soon as a new key exchange happens in the background. It basically heals itself.</v>

23
00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:57.431
<v Maya>Which is brilliant. But I mean, we have background papers in our stack today, right? Like Bhargavan and colleagues from 2024, they verified the PQX DH design. And Shival and his team earlier in 2026 did the first full</v>

24
00:02:57.572 --> 00:03:01.515
<v Maya>automated analysis of the double ratchet. So didn't they already prove this was secure?</v>

25
00:03:02.146 --> 00:03:11.611
<v Daniel>Well, yes and no. They proved that the abstract models were secure. They verify the design. They did not verify the actual code that runs on your phone.</v>

26
00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:13.232
<v Maya>Right. There's a huge difference there.</v>

27
00:03:13.392 --> 00:03:25.559
<v Daniel>A massive difference. That gap between design and reality is where all the really nasty bugs hide. Those prior papers were incredible work, but alpha proof code tackles the unmodified compiled code.</v>

28
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.944
<v Maya>We're talking about Libsignal version 0.105.4, right? With the SPQR crate?</v>

29
00:03:31.024 --> 00:03:38.410
<v Daniel>Yes. And specifically, it's compiled by the standard Rust compiler into a Arch64 Linux machine code.</v>

30
00:03:38.763 --> 00:03:42.886
<v Maya>Aarch 64 being what basically runs on every modern smartphone.</v>

31
00:03:43.166 --> 00:03:50.551
<v Daniel>Exactly. So to use an analogy, it's like, well, those prior papers proved that the blueprint for a bank vault was perfectly secure.</v>

32
00:03:50.751 --> 00:03:51.451
<v Maya>Okay, I like that.</v>

33
00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:59.536
<v Daniel>But alpha proof code is coming in and proving that the actual poured concrete and welded steel of the vault on the construction site is secure.</v>

34
00:03:59.757 --> 00:04:08.162
<v Maya>Right. Because even if the blueprint is perfect, the construction crew, which in this case is the Rust compiler, they might, you know, mess up the concrete mix or leave a gap.</v>

35
00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:14.673
<v Daniel>Exactly. By verifying the compiled machine code, they bypass any bugs introduced by the compiler entirely.</v>

36
00:04:14.951 --> 00:04:21.276
<v Maya>Wow. Okay, so how does this AI swarm actually pull this off? Because reading the paper, the workflow is just wild.</v>

37
00:04:21.576 --> 00:04:27.981
<v Daniel>It's an incredible engineering feat. So first, they had to create a formal semantics of AR64.</v>

38
